  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn H5 trof axis will move across the region through today from\nwest to east while becoming negatively tiled as a sfc low tracks\nto our west over Quebec. A sfc warm front will lift northward\nthis morning, placing much of the region within a broad warm\nsector with deep S-SW flow. Forcing for ascent will increase\nthrough today with precipitable water increasing up to around\n2.00\", which is around +2 standard deviations. This combined\nwith relatively deep warm cloud depths to around 13 kft will \nallow for embedded downpours within any showers and \nthunderstorms. Some backbuilding/training is also possible with \nrelatively short corfidi vectors. Despite this, the rainfall \nwill be beneficial in most locations as outside of portions of \nextreme southern NH, most areas remain quite dry. Given the \nlarge storage capacity of area rivers, river flooding is not \nexpected but some localized nuisance poor drainage flooding \ncannot be ruled out where heavy rainfall does occur. There is \nalso a nonzero threat for an isolated flash flood but coverage \nshould be limited in scope. Generally expecting up to around 2\" \nof rainfall on localized scales but locally higher amounts are \npossible. \n\nThe other thing to watch will be for possible strong to\nmarginally severe thunderstorms during the afternoon as the cold\nfront crosses. The main question is whether earlier day rain is\nable to clear in time for enough destabilization to occur but\nmost CAMs do show modest MLCAPE to develop of between 500-1000\nJ/kg, which will be collocated with up to 45 kts of 0-6 km bulk\nshear. The greatest threat for a strong storm will be across NH\nwith locally damaging winds the primary threat. \n\nIt will otherwise be a muggy day with high temperatures into the\n70s to near 80 degrees. Low temperatures tonight will mainly be\ninto the 50s with patchy fog possible. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nTuesday looks mostly dry as a ridge builds in behind a \ndeparting low pressure system. Temperatures should be able to \nclimb into the low to mid 80s south of the mountains, with mid \nto upper 70s to the north, as skies quickly clear out. Mostly \nclear skies persist through the evening with calming wind, so we\nshould see a cooler night with temperatures falling into the \nmid to upper 50s. Wednesday should be similar as the ridge axis \nmoves overhead with slightly warmer temperatures. The ridge axis\nmoves off to the east Wednesday night with a trough becoming \nestablished over the Great Lakes Region. This is going to re-\nestablish the southwesterly flow and once again advect more \nmoisture into the region. However, this time the strengthening \nBermuda High is going to largely keep the lower levels dry, so \nthis likely only results in increased humidity. The ridge axis \nstays off to the east so the building heat won't get too out of \nhand, but the latter half of the week could see temperatures \napproaching the 90F degree mark each afternoon and with elevated\ndewpoints it is going to feel a bit warmer. This is going to \nmake nighttime temperatures warm too as they can only cool to \nthe dewpoints which look to be in the mid to upper 60s. Global \nmodels are in good agreement that the 500 mb flow becomes more \nzonal Sunday which may be a turning point for the heat and \nhumidity, but that being at the end of the forecast we will have\nto watch the trends. \n\nPrecipitation chances won't be nil, but they are looking like \nthey will be sparse with not much in the way of forcing. Global \nmodels suggest there could be some shortwave energy moving \nthrough the broader trough late week, but this signal has been \ninconsistent as of late so it is hard to say if we will see \nanything more than just spotty diurnal showers and thunderstorms\nat this point. Many locations are likely to see dry conditions \ncontinue.",
